laurel 2158: zero-copy GPU surfaces with the stage-1 tables, and cache upkeep once per CPU write instead of per submission.
Boot 07:45 (2154 + 2157, stage 1 on) [V logs/usblog-20261101-001446.txt]: (1) "-attachImage: texture 4 is IOSurface 1 (BGRA
720 x 1560, 2880 bytes a row): a copy": with the tables RedwoodGPU took only surfaces whose IOBufferMemoryDescriptor holds 64
guard rows; surface 1 (the boot framebuffer 0x5c000000, physical) has none, so every 2nd frame was read back with glReadPixels
(31-55 ms "copying out"). (2) "13400-16648 KB out of the cache in 5283-15897 us a submission", up to 400 submissions in 5 s:
the "push again after 4 ms" rule ran on every submission because the push itself took longer than 4 ms ("handing on" up to
33 ms a frame). (3) "LAUREL GPU SMMU: fault FSR 00000400" every 5 s: FSR.FORMAT, no fault bit.
Changes: RedwoodGPU maps the scratch page (TopazAdreno redwood_gpu_map_scratch) after every stage-1 surface that has no guard
rows of its own (and the rest of the 64 KiB VA granule); a wrapped picture's buffer offset includes wrapDelta (2154 gave the
page start); a surface whose maps are all write-combined (SweetSurface noCache: the physical framebuffer) is never pushed;
kernel surfaces are pushed when the seed moved (a CPU write lock let go) or after 250 ms (writers that do not lock), wrapped
client memory at most once per 16 ms, measured from the end of the push; a VA = PA (GPU pool, write-combined) GEM buffer
is never pushed (stage 1 sets gpu_cached=1 for scattered buffers, so 2154 pushed every buffer Mesa had mapped, pool ones
included, at every submission: most of the 13-16 MB); new request 0x7d (kRedwoodDrawn): the GPU drew a
surface, its seed moves without a push; CPU_PREP waits for the buffer's own last submission; the SMMU fault line needs a
fault bit. Kill switch gpuseed=0 (the old 4 ms push rule for every surface). Pass-through (gpu_smmu=0) is unchanged.
New stats: "..., N surface pushes, M passed over (no CPU write since the last)" at the end of the RedwoodGPU 5 s line.

